{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on a513ae16: apply the v2 patch named in b5b42e33, then run the unchanged gate once at the repaired head. No limited pipeline.\n\nThe eight edits match R3. A due entry now waits for its alarm, so a witness that jumps the clock must run that alarm. I accept them as described, including the CLI wording \"next alarm\" and the new device-command case for the release-withdrawal precedence. Name that precedence change in the implementation note.\n\nOne condition applies to each alarm witness. Show that the host scheduled the alarm the test runs: use the runner's own result that an alarm ran, or read the stored alarm time. A bare call to the alarm handler is not enough, because it would pass even if production never scheduled the alarm. If any of the six stories has no scheduled alarm at its due time, stop and report. That would be a defect in R3, not a witness repair.\n\nScope: docs/testing.md allows a targeted follow-up only for an isolated repair to one test file, with production code unchanged. This repair touches six test files and packages/cli/src/verify-cleanup.ts, so it needs the gate. The gate took about 18 minutes at 22b5f36f. A frozen limited pipeline needs its own closure file and ledger, which the checker would also have to review. Run `npm run gate` once, with the original worker limits and deadlines.\n\nKeep the failed gate at 22b5f36f, manifest afe7dbac, recorded as failed. If the new gate fails, stop and report before any change. After R3 lands, I deploy it to all four Workers, including artroom-scope.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}